# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-19
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This tutorial reproduces the error-detected, approximate preparation of a W state by using dynamic circuits, as presented in [Smith, Pokharel, Maurya, Takita, \"Resilience Beyond the Light Cone: Error-Detected Primitives for Practical Dynamic Circuits\" (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08925) [\\[1\\]](#references).\n"
msgstr "Questo tutorial riproduce la preparazione approssimata di uno stato W con rilevamento degli errori tramite circuiti dinamici, come presentata in [Smith, Pokharel, Maurya, Takita, \"Resilience Beyond the Light Cone: Error-Detected Primitives for Practical Dynamic Circuits\" (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08925) [\\[1\\]](#references).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-19
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The approach is an adaptation of the algorithm presented in [Piroli, Styliaris, Cirac, \"Approximating many-body quantum states with quantum circuits and measurements\" (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.07604) [\\[2\\]](#references), upgraded with the *Distributed Control Error Detection* (DisCo ED) scheme of [Smith et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08925) The paper runs the protocol on a heavy-hex processor. Here, the protocol runs on the square lattice of a Nighthawk processor. The noisy simulation reproduces its main finding: both DisCo ED checks improve the prepared state compared with no error detection, and the implicit check gives the best state. On hardware at $n = 10$, the idle time during the feedforward limits how much error detection can recover, as discussed after the hardware results.\n"
msgstr "L'approccio è un adattamento dell'algoritmo presentato in [Piroli, Styliaris, Cirac, \"Approximating many-body quantum states with quantum circuits and measurements\" (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.07604) [\\[2\\]](#references), potenziato con lo schema *Distributed Control Error Detection* (DisCo ED) di [Smith et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08925) L'articolo esegue il protocollo su un processore heavy-hex. Qui, il protocollo viene eseguito sul reticolo quadrato di un processore Nighthawk. La simulazione rumorosa riproduce il suo risultato principale: entrambi i controlli DisCo ED migliorano lo stato preparato rispetto all'assenza di rilevamento degli errori, e il controllo implicito fornisce lo stato migliore. Su hardware con $n = 10$, il tempo di inattività durante il feedforward limita quanto il rilevamento degli errori possa recuperare, come discusso dopo i risultati su hardware.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-19
